<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/water-ceiling.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/water-ceiling-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/water-ceiling.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/appraiser-services/">Services</a><span>/</span>Water Damage Appraisal</nav><span class="kicker light fade-up">Appraisal service</span><h1 class="fade-up" style="animation-delay:.06s">Water Losses From Pipes, Roofs and Monsoon Rain</h1><p class="lede fade-up" style="animation-delay:.14s">A burst supply line and a monsoon leak through a parapet both soak drywall, flooring and cabinets, but they raise different policy questions. Our New Mexico water damage appraisal work values the repair once the carrier has accepted the loss.</p><div class="btn-row fade-up" style="animation-delay:.22s;margin-top:26px"><a class="btn gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn light" href="/contact-new-mexico-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts fade-up" style="animation-delay:.3s"><div><small>Common sources</small><b>Supply lines, water heaters, roofs</b></div><div><small>Monsoon</small><b>Roughly July–September</b></div><div><small>Not covered</small><b>Flood under standard forms</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Where water losses come from</h2>
<p>Sudden and accidental discharge from plumbing is the classic covered water loss: a failed supply line under a sink, a water heater that gives way in a closet, a washing machine hose that splits. In New Mexico, two more sources show up often. Monsoon storms push water through flat roofs at parapets, scuppers and canales, and winter freezes in higher elevations split pipes in exterior walls and crawlspaces (see <a href="/appraiser-services/winter-freeze-damage-appraisal/">winter freeze damage appraisal</a>).</p>
<h2>Monsoon roof leaks</h2>
<p>Flat-roofed Pueblo Revival and Territorial homes rely on parapets, crickets and canales to move water off the roof. When a heavy monsoon cell dumps rain faster than the drains handle it, water can pond against a parapet and find a cracked stucco cap or a failed seam. Interior damage follows: stained vigas, wet insulation, swollen drywall and buckled wood floors.</p>
<p>Policies often distinguish between damage caused by a covered storm and damage from long-term deterioration. Carriers may pay the interior damage but not the roof, or the reverse. The panel can value both pieces; deciding what is covered is a separate question.</p>
<h2>Flood is a different policy</h2>
<p>Standard New Mexico homeowners and commercial property policies exclude flood, meaning surface water, overflow of bodies of water and mudflow. Monsoon flash flooding in arroyos and low-lying streets is common, especially below burn scars, but damage from rising surface water is usually not covered by a homeowners policy. Flood coverage is typically purchased separately, often through the National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP) or a private flood policy.</p>
<ul>
<li>NFIP policies have their own claim rules, proof-of-loss deadlines and appraisal provisions that differ from homeowners forms.</li>
<li>One storm can create both a covered roof leak and an excluded surface-water loss in the same house. Separating them carefully matters.</li>
<li>If you are unsure whether a loss is flood or a covered water event, ask your carrier in writing and consider speaking with a New Mexico attorney.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What we value on a water claim</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Mitigation and drying.</strong> Water extraction, equipment days, antimicrobial treatment and containment, compared with industry drying standards.</li>
<li><strong>Tear-out and rebuild.</strong> Drywall and plaster, insulation, baseboards, doors, cabinets and countertops.</li>
<li><strong>Flooring.</strong> Tile, wood and carpet, including whether undamaged rooms must be replaced for a continuous floor.</li>
<li><strong>Source repair access.</strong> Opening walls or slabs to reach the failed pipe, even when the pipe itself may not be covered.</li>
<li><strong>Contents.</strong> Furniture, rugs, electronics and stored items.</li>
<li><strong>Additional living expense</strong> where the home is unlivable during repairs.</li>
</ol>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Mold limits</b>Many policies cap mold remediation at a set dollar amount or exclude it unless it results from a covered water loss. Read that provision early, because it can shape the estimate.</div>
<h3>Timing and documentation</h3>
<p>Water losses get worse with time, and policies usually require reasonable steps to protect the property. Keep receipts from plumbers and mitigation companies, photograph moisture readings, and save removed materials until the carrier has a chance to inspect. Our <a href="/claim-estimators/claim-calendar-builder/">claim calendar builder</a> helps track deadlines, and the <a href="/claim-estimators/appraisal-file-checkup/">appraisal file checkup</a> flags missing records.</p>
<h3>What makes a New Mexico water damage appraisal different</h3>
<p>Adobe walls, plaster over lath, viga ceilings and saltillo tile do not dry or replace like standard drywall and vinyl plank. A New Mexico water damage appraisal has to price those materials honestly, including the specialty trades they need, rather than defaulting to the cheapest modern equivalent.</p>
<h2>Getting started</h2>
<p>If the carrier has paid for drying but not for the rebuild, or has priced a single wall when the whole room is affected, appraisal may help. Start with a <a href="/appraiser-services/claim-estimate-review/">claim estimate review</a> or <a href="/contact-new-mexico-appraiser/">contact us</a> with your file. Look up terms like “sudden and accidental” in <a href="/claim-terms-a-to-z/">claim terms A to Z</a>.</p>

<h2>Questions about water damage appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is monsoon flash flooding covered by homeowners insurance?</summary><div class="a"><p>Usually not. Standard homeowners policies exclude flood and surface water; separate flood coverage, such as an NFIP policy, is typically required.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can appraisal decide if my leak was sudden or gradual?</summary><div class="a"><p>That is mainly a coverage question. The panel values the damage; disagreements about whether the leak is covered may need a New Mexico attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>Should I throw out wet drywall right away?</summary><div class="a"><p>Remove it to prevent further damage if needed, but photograph it and keep samples or pieces until the carrier has had a chance to see them.</p></div></details></div>
